  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of 'sustainable development' was first introduced in the 1980s by the World Commission on Environment and Development, led by Gro Harlem Brundtland. The commission's report, 'Our Common Future', highlighted the need for a balance between economic growth, social justice, and environmental protection.\n\nIn recent years, sustainable development has become a key concept in international relations, with many countries integrating it into their national policies. However, critics argue that the emphasis on economic growth often leads to environmental degradation and social inequality.\n\nThe Brundtland Commission's definition of sustainable development is still widely accepted today: 'meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs'. This concept has been applied in various fields, including agriculture, energy production, and urban planning.\n\nDespite its importance, sustainable development remains a complex issue. It requires a multidisciplinary approach, involving governments, businesses, and civil society organizations working together to achieve common goals. The challenge lies in finding a balance between competing interests and priorities.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What was the main idea of the World Commission on Environment and Development's report?\",\n      \"options\": [\"To promote economic growth at any cost\", \"To protect the environment without considering social justice\", \"To find a balance between economic growth, social justice, and environmental protection\", \"To blame governments for environmental degradation\"],\n      \"answer\": \"c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"What is meant by 'meeting the needs of the present' in the Brundtland Commission's definition?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Providing basic necessities like food and water\", \"Achieving economic growth and prosperity\", \"Protecting the environment for future generations\", \"Increasing social inequality\"],\n      \"answer\": \"c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"According to the text, what is a challenge in implementing sustainable development?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Finding common goals among governments and businesses\", \"Achieving economic growth without environmental degradation\", \"Increasing social inequality and environmental protection\", \"Funding sustainable projects\"],\n      \"answer\": \"a\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What is the author's attitude towards sustainable development?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Critical, highlighting its limitations and challenges\", \"Supportive, emphasizing its importance and benefits\", \"Neutral, providing a balanced view of its pros and cons\", \"Ambivalent, questioning its relevance in modern times\"],\n      \"answer\": \"c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"What is implied by the phrase 'environmental protection' in the context of sustainable development?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Reducing carbon emissions and climate change\", \"Protecting endangered species and their habitats\", \"Preserving natural resources for future generations\", \"Increasing pollution and waste\"],\n      \"answer\": \"c\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
